
                       FEATURES OF VOLKSWRITER 3

              FOR USERS OF EARLIER VERSIONS OF VOLKSWRITER



This document is for those who are familiar with an earlier Volks
writer.  We want to introduce you to the new features of Volkswriter 3
in terms you will find familiar -- by relating them to the features of
the earlier versions.


                      WHAT'S NEW AT THE MAIN MENU

The options you see at the Main Menu have been rearranged.  Two of the
old options have been removed:  Format Selection is now reached with F9
during editing, and Help is now reached by pressing F1 at the Main Menu.
Two new options have been added - Style Sheets and Utilities.

The D option, formerly "Display All Files", is now called Document
Directory.  It still displays the files on the specified drive or subdi
rectory, but you can also use DOS wildcards (* and ?) to see only a spe
cific group of your files (see your DOS manual).  Subdirectories are
indicated with angle brackets < > around them (see Chapter 2).

The Retrieve function has been renamed Revise Document, but there is no
change in the way you use it.



                       FINDING THE RIGHT FUNCTION


All the capability you found in Volkswriter Deluxe - and much more - is
here in Volkswriter 3, but you may find it in a different place.  Here's
a guide to help you find familiar functions:

..LAYOUT 2  
1.  Ctrl-F1 previously brought up the Format Selection Menu.  These
    options (and more, as we'll see) are now part of the Layouts Menu,
    which you reach by pressing F9.  Ctrl-F1 now takes you to a new menu
    of "preferences."

2.  F9 is the key you used with Deluxe to change tabs or margins on the
    "ruler line."  The ruler line is now part of the layout, so F9 still
    takes you there; just press F9 and then T (for Tabs and Margins).

..LAYOUT 3  
    It's easy to make a change in tabs or margins:  First, move the cur
    sor to the line where you want the new tabs or margins to go into
    effect.  Press F9 and then press T to get to the ruler line.  Set
    the margins and tabs you want, and then press F9 twice.  That's all
    there is to it.

    Your new margins and tabs go into effect from the line where the
    cursor was when you pressed F9.  If you want to set margins and tabs
    for the entire document, just move the cursor to Line 1 before you
    press F9.  All changes of tabs or margins will be stored with the
    document, so you don't need to think about associated formats any
    more.

    We'll say more about converting your old Volkswriter documents to
    Volkswriter 3 documents below.  To learn about layouts in detail,
    use the Revise function to retrieve TUTOR3, and see Chapter 6 in the
    manual.

3.  F2, which you used for storing documents, is now the Spelling key.
    To store a partial document, use Ctrl-F10.  If you have a block of
    text marked in your document and press Ctrl-F10, you will be asked
    to confirm that you want to store only the block.

    To store your entire document, we recommend that you press Alt-F10,
    to exit to the Main Menu, and S to Store.  If you want to continue
    editing, pressing E now takes you back to the place in the document
    where you were, instead of to the beginning of the file (see Store
    in Chapter 4, and Storing Part of a File in Chapter 8).



..LAYOUT 1  
                             NEW FUNCTIONS


..LAYOUT 2  
1.  Ctrl-F9 is used for math and sorting, which you can do at any time
    while editing.  The general procedure is to mark the column(s) or
    row(s) with Ctrl-F5 and Ctrl-F6; then press F9 and select the func
    tion you want (see TUTOR5 and Chapter 9).

2.  Spelling uses the F2 key.  Press F2 once to check a single word at
    the cursor -- the word you have just typed, for example.  Press F2 a
    second time to check continuously from the cursor line down, or to
    check a marked block (see TUTOR5 and Chapter 5).

3.  Hyphenation occurs automatically, whenever words are "wrapped" at
    the right margin, with no interruption of your work.

4.  F9 is the layout key, discussed above.  Three new ruler-line symbols
    let you control indenting/outdenting of the first line of para
    graphs; control the hyphenation zone, which determines how long a
    word must be before it is a candidate for hyphenation; and let you
    enter decimal tabs (see TUTOR3 and Chapter 6).

5.  Reformatting now occurs automatically, when you make a change in a
    paragraph and then move the cursor.  We'll discuss reformatting in a
    moment.

..page


..LAYOUT 1  
                       FAMILIAR FEATURES ENHANCED

..LAYOUT 2  
1.  Ctrl-F3, formerly Proof, is now "Marks" in the F1 Help menu.  It
    allows you to turn on or off display of special effects markers. The
    markers can remain turned off while you work (see Show/Hide Markers
    in Chapter 4).

..LAYOUT 3  
    You will find it a pleasure to work with the markers hidden.  Refor
    matting now stops at a blank line, so you don't need to check for
    paragraph-end symbols to see where reformatting will stop.  Turn on
    the markers if you need to check the placement of symbols, or to
    check which font you have selected.

    Certain functions require you to have markers hidden.  Math func
    tions do not work with markers displayed.  You should also have the
    markers turned off when marking a block and setting bold, shadow,
    underlining, overstrike, or font selections (otherwise you may get
    extra special-effect markers).

    To delete a paragraph-end symbol, you first turn the markers on
    (except in the special case of joining two paragraphs, described
    below), and then delete the symbol.  If you want to delete a marker,
    turn markers on with Ctrl-F3, delete symbols as desired, and then
    turn markers off by pressing Ctrl-F3 again.


..LAYOUT 2  
2.  Automatic reformatting takes care of nearly all reformatting of
    paragraphs you will need.  Automatic reformatting does not delete
    leading spaces on the first line of a paragraph.

..LAYOUT 3  
    The F8 key is still available if you need to reformat manually.  F8
    follows exactly the margins in the layouts in your document, so it
    does delete leading spaces on the first line of a paragraph.  By
    using the # symbol in the ruler line, you can specify indentation if
    you want it for the first lines of your paragraphs.

    F8 will also reformat text you have entered with a temporary left
    margin using Shift-Tab.  However, since you can have different left
    margins in different parts of your document, you should seldom need
    Shift-Tab.

    As noted, reformatting with F8 now stops at a blank line, like auto
    matic reformatting.

    You can also mark part or all of your text with F5 and F6, and press
    F8 to reformat the marked block (global reformatting). 

    If you want to join two paragraphs, place the cursor a space or two
    after the last character in the first paragraph (use the F3 key to
    get there) and press F8.  The first paragraph must end with a para
    graph-end symbol, and there must not be a layout change after the
    first paragraph.  The second paragraph will be joined to the first.
..LAYOUT 2  

3.  Column functions have been enhanced.  Columns are used for math and
    sorting.  The moving, copying, and deleting functions have changed
    as follows:

    Column Move or Copy Horizontally - If you place the cursor on the
    same line as the Ctrl-F5 marker (a horizontal move or copy), the
    marked column is removed from its original placement and inserted at
    the cursor position.  Text or columns at the destination are pushed
    to the right to make room.  No reformatting occurs.

    Column Move or Copy Vertically - If you place the cursor on a dif
    ferent line than the Ctrl-F5 marker (a vertical move or copy), the
    marked column is removed from its original placement, and it over
    writes the old information at the destination.  No reformatting
    occurs, and text at the destination is not pushed to the right.

    Column Delete - After marking the column and pressing Alt-F8 to
    delete the block, you will now be asked whether you want to delete
    the space occupied by the column ("Delete"), or replace the column
    with blanks ("Blank").

4.  You can now move more quickly through the menus.  You no longer have
    to move the cursor down through a list of options to reach the one
    you want to change, as you did with the old Format menu.  For those
    options with Yes/No (Y/N) answers, simply press the corresponding
    letter to "toggle" the selection from Yes to No, or from No to Yes.
    The cursor only moves to those options that require you to enter
    information, such as character size.

5.  Centering, Alt-F9, has been enhanced.  Pressing Alt-F9 on a line
    centers it, and automatically places a paragraph symbol and a spe
    cial centering marker at the end of the line.  A centered line will
    not be moved by reformatting.  To "un-center" a centered line, place
    the cursor to the left of the text, where you want the text aligned,
    and press F4.

6.  The F4 key now deletes leading blanks before a word, if there are
    any, without deleting the word.  If you press F4 again, it deletes
    one word at a time including any trailing blanks, just as before.
    Once  you have deleted all the words on a line to the right of the
    cursor, pressing F4 again causes the line to reformat.  Then conti
    nuing to press F4 will continue deleting words and blanks as usual.
..LAYOUT 3  

    F4 also "un-centers" a centered line if the cursor is in the space
    to the left of the text.
..LAYOUT 2  

7.  A block or column you mark is now displayed in reverse video.

8.  Block movements now cause reformatting at origin and destination, so
    your text will conform to the layouts in effect at both locations.

..LAYOUT 3  
9.  You can turn on or off special printing effects (such as boldface or
    underlining) for a marked block.  This makes it easy to change
    printing effects for several paragraphs at once.

..LAYOUT 1  

                            THERE'S MORE...

To Stop or Cancel a Function:  You can now interrupt certain functions
that you were not able to stop previously.  If you press F10 during
spelling verification (F2), global reformatting (F8), replace (Alt-F7),
or revise layout (choice R from the Layout Menu), you cancel the func
tion.  Pressing any other key will display a message asking if you want
to "Cancel?" or "Stop?".  Pressing Enter is the same as answering No to
the question, and the function will resume.  If you cancel reformatting,
be sure to check your text.  Correct it and use F8 to reformat that
paragraph or line.


Headers and Footers:  With Volkswriter 3, you can have a different
header or footer on the same line of odd and even pages.  The headers in
the manual are an example.  Your old ..head and ..foot commands will
work without change. Note that options such as lines per inch and char
acter size in headers and footers throughout your document are governed
by Layout 1.


Ctrl-U:  The Ctrl-U (section symbol) no longer functions as a page
break.  If you used Ctrl-U in your documents, it is easy to replace them
with ␀..PAGE commands:  Press Alt-F7.  At the "REPLACE: "  prompt, press
Ctrl-U.  At the "WITH: " prompt, enter "..page".  Answer "Y" to the ALL?
prompt.


Printing in Boldface or Shadowprint:  Two options that were available
with formats have been removed from the layout menu: BOLD and SHADOW.
With some printers, these were used to get the highest quality printout.
You can still print your document in boldface or shadow: mark the entire
document with F5 and F6, and then press Shift-F7, to select Boldface, or
Shift-F10 for shadowprint.

..LAYOUT 3  


                     USING YOUR OLD VOLKSWRITER FORMATS

..LAYOUT 1  

Volkswriter 3 will automatically convert the old formats that go with
your Volkswriter Deluxe document into layouts.  This will happen auto
matically when you retrieve your Volkswriter Deluxe file that has an
associated format.  There are only two requirements: the Volkswriter
Deluxe file must be a standard document that includes paragraph-end sym
bols, and the format file(s) must be on the same disk and directory as
the file.

When you retrieve one of these files, its associated format becomes Lay
out 1 of the document.  Any other formats specified within the document
with ␀..FORMAT commands become the other layouts of the document, up to
the total of 15 possible different layouts.  When you store the docu
ment, all the layouts will be stored with it permanently, and it will
then be a Volkswriter 3 document.

To convert formats to layouts for new Volkswriter 3 files: If you have
Volkswriter Deluxe formats that you want to continue using for new
documents you create with Volkswriter 3, you can convert them to "style
sheets."  All you do is retrieve one of the Deluxe files that uses the
formats, as above.  Then exit to the Main Menu and select the L option
to make a style sheet.  Indicate the extension that you want this style
sheet applied to.

When you do this, Volkswriter 3 will automatically use the style sheet
in the future whenever you create a new document with that extension.
It is similar to Volkswriter Deluxe's use of associated formats, except
that the style sheet is a starting point for a new document when you
create it; once you store the document, all the layout information is
also stored, and that document no longer refers to the style sheet.  The
style sheet will also be used when you retrieve a Volkswriter Deluxe
document that has the same extension (unless there are embedded
␀..FORMAT commands in the document; in that case, no style sheet will be
used, only the formats).



                       CREATING PLAIN TEXT FILES


Volkswriter 3 adds layout information to your documents.  When you want
a plain DOS file, for use with another program, you can create one by
using a * to mark the right margin in Layout 1 of your document.  Such a
file will not contain layout information, but otherwise can contain all
256 ASCII characters (see Chapter 8). To create a plain ASCII file, with
only the standard character set (from ASCII 32 to ASCII 127), use the
"export" option of the Utilities Menu (Chapter 10).


..page

                       CUSTOMIZED PRINTER TABLES


NOTE:  SKIP THE FOLLOWING SECTION UNLESS YOU HAVE A VOLKSWRITER DELUXE
CUSTOMIZED PRINTER TABLE THAT YOU WANT TO USE WITH VOLKSWRITER 3.


Enhancements and changes have been made to many of the existing Volks
writer Deluxe printer tables, and many new printer tables have been
added.  We have expanded our documentation to include a better way of
determining which printer table number you should use in your layouts.
See Appendix D.

Please try the new printer table that is suggested for your printer.
If, after trying the new printer table, you still want to use a custom
printer table from Volkswriter Deluxe, do the following:

..LAYOUT 2  
1.  Use the Printer Table filename chart in Appendix D to determine the
    filename of your custom printer table.  Go to the DOS prompt on your
    computer.

2.  FOR DISKETTE SYSTEMS

..LAYOUT 3  
    Place your old working Volkswriter Deluxe program diskette in Drive
    A and place your new working Volkswriter 3 Program 2 diskette in
    Drive B.

    Set the default drive to A by typing  A:  and pressing the Enter
    key.  Type the following commands, and press Enter after each one:

    COPY A:oldfilename.TBL B:VWPRnnn.TBL
    COPY A:oldfilename.PRP B:VWPRnnn.PRP

    where     oldfilename is the filename of your Volkswriter Deluxe
                          custom printer table

                   nnn    is the printer type number

    NOTE:  You will not have a .PRP table unless your printer supports
    proportional spacing.



    FOR HARD DISK SYSTEMS

    Set your current directory to your old Volkswriter 3 subdirectory by
    using the appropriate CD\ command.  This examples assumes that your
    hard disk is Drive C; if it is not, substitute the appropriate drive
    specification.

    Type the following commands and press Enter after each one:

    COPY C:oldfilename.TBL \newvw3dir\VWPRnnn.TBL
    COPY C:oldfilename.PRP \newvw3dir\VWPRnnn.PRP

    where    oldfilename  is the filename of your Volkswriter Deluxe
                          custom printer table

             newvw3dir    the new subdirectory that contains your Volks
                          writer 3 program files

                    nnn   is the printer type number 

    NOTE:  You will not have a .PRP table unless your printer supports
    proportional spacing.

..LAYOUT 1  
This procedure will make your old custom table available with Volks
writer 3 with the same printer number.  This will eliminate the need to
change printer numbers in your layouts.  If the printer number that you
used was one found in the VW3 printer filename list, this procedure
overwrites that printer table on your working VW3 disk.


CHANGING THE DEFAULT PRINTER NUMBER

If you want to change the default printer number, do the following:

..LAYOUT 2  
1.  At the VW3 main menu, press C for Create a document.  Create a file
    with an extension of .LYT.  For example, C.LYT.  This guarantees
    that you will activate the universal style sheet (VWSTYLE.LYT).

2.  Press the F9 key, and select D for Document Options, then T for
    Printer Type.  Enter the desired printer number and press Enter.
    Then F9 to set it, F10 to exit the Layout menu.

3.  Press Alt-F10 to exit to the Main Menu, press L for Style Sheet.
    Choose U to create a Universal style sheet.


ACCESSING THE NEW PRINTER TABLE FROM CONFIGUR

1.  In order to access your custom table with Option 3 of the CONFIGUR
    program you will have to modify the list of printers that is used by
    CONFIGUR.  This file is called VWPRCONF.TBL and is located in a sub
    directory called PRINTERS on your working Volkswriter 3 INSTALLATION
    disk.

2.  From the Volkswriter 3 Main Menu, press D (Document Directory), and
    enter the following at the DRIVE, PATH, or DOCUMENT GROUP prompt:

..LAYOUT 3  
    d:\PRINTERS

    where d = the drive that contains the Installation Disk

..LAYOUT 2  
3.  Press F10 to cancel the directory display, then press R for Revise
    Document.  Enter the filename: VWPRCONF.TBL.

4.  Press Ctrl-End to move to the end of the file.  You will see several
    lines labeled RESERVED FOR LIFETREE USE.

5.  Place the cursor on the first of these lines, and with Insert off,
    type the name of your printer.  In Columns 38 through 40, enter the
    printer number you are using for this printer.  Be sure that the
    last digit of your printer number is in Column 40.

6.  Press Alt-F10 to exit and press S to store the file under the same
    name.

7.  The next time you run Option 3 of the CONFIGUR program, your custom
    printer table will appear at the end of the printer list.

..LAYOUT 1  
WARNING:  Do NOT change ANY of the numbers at the top of this file, or
alter any other lines besides the RESERVED lines.  Do not use the INSERT
or DELETE keys when editing this file.
